	Here is a list of the changes made to the various NetHack 2.3
files which I originally ftp'd from uunet.UU.NET (in the nethack2.3
subdirectory of /comp.sources.games) in order to make the game compile
using Turbo C 2.0.  I have decided to include *all* changes I made,
including some which probably do not make any difference at all, but
are in my code from when I was trying just about *anything* to try and
get NetHack to compile correctly.  I believe I have included
everything, but I may have inadvertently left out something essential.
Let me know if you have any problems with it.  Some of the fixes may
not be elegant, but hey, it works! :-)

	-In config.h, edit so that MSDOS is defined, UNIX, BSD, MAIL,
	DEF_MAILREADER, SECURE and MAILFREQ *not* defined, HACKDIR is
	defined as "c:\games\nethackdir".  Leave everything else as it
	is.

	-Move the lines
		#ifdef MSDOSCOLOR
			endscreen();
		#endif
	in end.c to just before the settty((char *) 0); on line 190 (to keep
	the score info from disappearing before you have a chance to read it,
	which happens if the endscreen() is left where it was).

	-In extern.h, add
		extern int ssignal();
		extern int gsignal();
	and include the ssignal.c code (which came with TC) to be compiled 
	with the rest of the files, or add ssignal.obj to a library and
	include it in the tlink command in the makefile.  I used the former
	technique.  This is needed only if you use TC 2.0 because it no
	longer supports ssignal and gsignal.
	Also change
		extern long *alloc();
	to
		extern void *alloc();
	in the same file.

	-Add the lines
		#ifdef DGK
			if (!count_only)
		#endif
	before the
		free((char *) mtmp);
	on line 169 in the file lev.c.

	-change the leading '/*' in msdos.c before "An assortment of MSDOS
	..." to ' *'.

	-add #include <stdlib.h> to msdos.h, then change the line
		char buf[BUFSIZ], *bp, *pp, *getenv(), lastch;
	to
		char buf[BUFSIZ], *bp, *pp, lastch;
	on line 717 of the same file.

	-in the file pcmain.c, change
		static void moveloop();
	to
		void moveloop();
	Also, check and see if the lines
		#ifdef WIZARD
			if (wizard && dlevel == 1)
		#ifdef STOOGES
		pline ("The wiz is at %d, the medusa is at %d, and the stooges
			are at %d", u.wiz_level, u.medusa_level, u.stooge_level);
		#else
			pline ("The wiz is at %d, and the medusa at %d",
				u.wiz_level, u.medusa_level);
		#endif
		#endif
	exist in the pcmain.c file.  I had to add them by yanking them from
	another main file, but I could have screwed up the file somehow before
	I added it to my "untouched" files archive, I suppose.  They should be
	just after line 277.  This won't affect the compile, but you won't get
	the announcement about the whereabouts of the wiz et al when the game
	starts up in Wizard mode.
	Last, I changed the line
		#endif /* CHDIR /**/
	to
		#endif /* CHDIR */
	near the end of pcmaini.c though I don't know if that made any 
	difference.

	-In the file rip.c, change the lines
		!strncmp(killer, "Mr.") ? "" :
		!strncmp(killer, "Ms.") ? "" :
	to
		!strncmp(killer, "Mr.", 3) ? "" :
		!strncmp(killer, "Ms.", 3) ? "" :

	-To fix the infamous blindfold / badge bug in steal.c that allows
	Nymphs to steal you item, but leave you blind and/or badged, add the
	following lines 
		if (otmp->otyp == BLINDFOLD && Blindfold && otmp->owornmask) {
			Blindfolded = 0;
			if (!Blinded)	Blinded = 1;	/* see on next move */
			else		pline("You still cannot see.");
			otmp->owornmask = 0;
		}
		if (otmp->otyp == BADGE && Badged %% otmp->owornmask) {
			Badged = 0;
			otmp->owornmask = 0;
		}
	just after the line (~ line 157)
		pline("%s stole %s", named ? "She" : Monnam(mtmp), doname(otmp));

	-In termcap.c, change
		extern long *alloc();
	to
		extern void *alloc();

	-In topten.c, add
		#include <dos.h>
	and
		#ifdef MSDOSCOLOR
		char nada
		#endif
	somewhere appropriate at the begining of the file.  I also changed
		char *lgfile = LOGFILE;
	to
		char *lgfile = "logfile";
	which probably does not change anything if LOGFILE is defined as
	"logfile" in config.h.  Add an #ifdef UNIXt before the
		while(link(lgfile, loglock) == -1) {
	and add an #endif /* UNIX */ just before the fopen(), which should be
	changed to read
		if(!(lfile = fopen(lgfile,"r"))) {
	(I don't know why the "a" append mode didn't work, but changing it to
	"r" stopped the "Cannot open logfile!" message I was getting at the
	end of each game, and I really didn't need the logfile anyhow.)

	The last changes are to the makefile, which I'll just attach here.

